    <!-- 06 Selection Framework -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="framework">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">06 &mdash; Framework</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The 3-circle niche selection framework</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The best niche sits at the intersection of skills, passion, and demand</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Missing any one circle leads to burnout, low quality, or no income</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Start with demand, then filter through skills and passion</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Don&rsquo;t just pick the highest-paying niche. Pick the one where you can actually win. The 3-circle framework helps you find that intersection.</p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3.5 mb-6">
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4">
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1.5 text-accent">Circle 1: Skills</h3>
            <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 leading-[1.5]">What are you actually good at? If you&rsquo;re great on camera but terrible at explaining software, tech UGC won&rsquo;t work no matter how well it pays. Be honest about your production skills, on-camera presence, subject-matter knowledge, and ability to learn quickly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4">
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1.5 text-accent">Circle 2: Passion</h3>
            <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 leading-[1.5]">What do you genuinely care about? UGC requires creating dozens of videos per month. If you&rsquo;re bored by the products, it shows. Authenticity is the whole point of UGC &mdash; audiences detect faked enthusiasm instantly. Your passion doesn&rsquo;t need to be extreme; mild genuine interest beats zero interest.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4">
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1.5 text-accent">Circle 3: Demand</h3>
            <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 leading-[1.5]">Are brands actually hiring for this? Check UGC platforms, job boards, and agency listings. If demand is low, even perfect skills and burning passion won&rsquo;t generate income. Start with demand, then filter through your skills and passion to find the overlap.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What happens when a circle is missing</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>Skills + Passion, no Demand:</strong> You create great content nobody will pay for. This is the hobbyist trap.</li>
          <li><strong>Skills + Demand, no Passion:</strong> You get hired but burn out within 3&ndash;6 months. Content quality declines. Brands don&rsquo;t renew.</li>
          <li><strong>Passion + Demand, no Skills:</strong> You want to create content and brands want it, but your deliverables don&rsquo;t meet the bar. Invest in skill-building first.</li>
          <li><strong>All three overlap:</strong> This is your niche. You enjoy the content, you&rsquo;re good at making it, and brands are paying for it.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The practical process</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>List 3&ndash;5 niches</strong> from the ranking table above that interest you.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Rate each niche 1&ndash;10</strong> on skills (can you create this content today?), passion (would you watch/consume this content yourself?), and demand (from the table above).</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Multiply the scores.</strong> The highest product is your best-fit niche. A 7&times;8&times;6 (336) beats a 10&times;10&times;2 (200) because demand is the bottleneck.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Create 5 sample videos</strong> in your top niche before committing. If making those 5 videos feels painful, the niche isn&rsquo;t right. If it feels natural, you&rsquo;ve found your lane.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07 Break Into a New Niche -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="break-in">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Breaking In</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How to break into a new niche</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Create 5&ndash;10 spec (unpaid) videos to build a niche-specific portfolio</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Study the top-performing ads in your target niche using ad libraries</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Position yourself as a specialist from day one, even with a small portfolio</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Switching niches &mdash; or entering one for the first time &mdash; requires a deliberate approach. You can&rsquo;t just add &ldquo;tech UGC&rdquo; to your bio and expect SaaS companies to hire you. You need proof of concept: niche-specific samples that demonstrate you understand the vertical&rsquo;s content language.</p>

        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Research the niche&rsquo;s ad library.</strong> Use Meta Ad Library and TikTok Creative Center to study top-performing ads in your target niche. Note the formats, hooks, scripts, and production styles that brands are currently running. This is your brief.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Create 5&ndash;10 spec videos.</strong> Pick real products from brands in your target niche. Create UGC-style videos as if you were hired. These don&rsquo;t need to be published &mdash; they&rsquo;re for your portfolio. Focus on matching the production level and content style you saw in the ad libraries.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Build a niche-specific portfolio page.</strong> Separate your niche work from your general portfolio. When you pitch a SaaS company, send them a link that shows only tech/SaaS content. Brands want to see niche fit, not versatility.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Pitch 20&ndash;30 brands.</strong> Cold outreach to brands in your target niche. Lead with your spec work. Offer a discounted first project (not free &mdash; discounted) to build the first paid case study. Once you have 3&ndash;5 paid projects, raise your rates to market level.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Update your positioning everywhere.</strong> Bio, website, portfolio, UGC platform profiles &mdash; all should reflect your niche specialization. &ldquo;UGC creator specializing in SaaS product demos&rdquo; converts far better than &ldquo;UGC creator.&rdquo;</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Multi-Niche vs Specialist -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="strategy">
